Plot filename random-state checks
=================================

  ./maxima-local --no-init --lisp=sbcl \
    --batch-string='run_testsuite(tests=[rtest_plotoptions]);'

Run every configured Lisp and the complete core+share suite. Eleven appended
examples preserve previous test numbers. Require the full count and inspect
unexpected errors; Maxima can catch Lisp errors and still exit zero.

RANDOM-NAME binds its fresh Common Lisp random state locally. Filename
character selection stays the same, while the caller retains its state
object and sequence on return or unwinding. The test helper compares the
next 32 random values with an untouched state copy. Tests cover empty and
negative counts, invalid counts, 128 generated lengths, native/fallback
jobs, and the actual Gnuplot script-name/path registration helper.

Maxima's user-visible MT19937 generator is separate and has its own
preservation check. A repeated-name smoke check catches accidentally cloning
the same unchanged caller state for every filename. Random names are not a
formal guarantee of uniqueness. The path test uses a private registration
table and writes no file; actual rendering is not part of that test.

This isolates filename generation's RNG. Shared temporary-file registration,
Gnuplot pipe access and draw's separate state require their own concurrency
checks. No mathematical plotting or simplification formula changes here.
